        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"colette-le-ble-en-herbe-6a5d5cd54d704a4a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Vinca directly presents her love for Phil as an established fact: “tu sais bien que je t'aime mieux que tout le monde.” The utterance is also a plea for reassurance and renewed communication, but it does not itself undertake a commitment. Her distress and urgency intensify the avowal without independently making the words an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The appeal invokes their existing bond and may have relationship-maintaining effects, but this supplies little or no performative undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Vinca directly addresses Phil and avows that she loves him more than anyone else.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Vinca says this while pleading with a jealous, withdrawn Phil not to remain angry and to speak to her. The formulation “tu sais bien” treats her love as already known and uses that avowed fact to seek reconciliation. The narrator subsequently describes her conduct in terms of the humility, awkwardness, and obstinacy of “véritable amour,” strongly framing the avowal as sincere. Her breathlessness, dismay, and hand-kissing convey intensity but do not show that the words escaped as a reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Phil, tu sais bien que je t'aime mieux que tout le monde.”\",\"supports\":\"The affirmative embedded proposition explicitly presents Vinca's loving state, with Phil cast as already knowing it; this strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je t'en prie, ne sois pas fâché... Tu as quelque chose contre moi... Parle-moi, Phil!”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate function is a pleading appeal for Phil to stop withdrawing and communicate, not an explicit commitment or relational undertaking; this supports low P.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator calls Vinca “consternée” and characterizes her humility, awkwardness, and obstinacy as those of “véritable amour.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration frames her avowal as grounded in genuine love while providing emotional delivery context rather than independent evidence of expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Vinca is breathless, kneels beside Phil, and kisses his hand before he pulls it away.\",\"supports\":\"These actions establish urgency and emotional intensity, but there is no indication that she blurts the words involuntarily or before reflection; therefore they do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its pleading, emotionally intense setting is adequately handled as context; no additional core-force category is required.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
